    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper evaluates unlearning on benchmarked targeted-knowledge tasks, but does not test recovery attacks such as relearning, activation ablation, quantization, or prompt rephrasing.",
    "gate_note": "The reported experiments compare unlearning methods on TOFU, WMDP, and MUSE; they do not apply any of the recovery attacks in the claim. Rows below record the WMDP targeted-knowledge accuracy results, for which the paper gives a chance reference. TOFU Forget Quality scores and MUSE memorization/generation scores are not accuracy-versus-chance measurements. The qualitative MUSE examples are noted under fits_no_version.",
    "test_reason": "This is a method paper. It proposes VILA, a LoRA and Fisher-variance initialization for unlearning, and reports its forgetting scores against FILA and plain LoRA baselines on TOFU, WMDP and MUSE. It applies no recovery attack of any kind: no relearning, no ablation or probing, no quantization, no rephrasing or jailbreaks. Its WMDP accuracies measure the method's own performance, not whether unlearning is shallow. Its selection protocol also excludes runs whose WMDP accuracy drops to the 0.25 chance level (Appendix J). So it cannot speak to version A's near-chance condition either. It is not a version F study, because it makes no claim of robustness to attacks.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly right. The authors conclude that VILA fixes FILA's Fisher-information estimate and computes the importance map from LoRA gradients only. They report up to 100× parameter efficiency and 40× faster training, and state-of-the-art forgetting on TOFU, WMDP and MUSE while preserving utility. They draw no conclusions about recovery attacks.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper proposes VILA, a modified Fisher-information and LoRA-based unlearning method; it compares against FILA but does not reproduce a recovery attack or the original sources' attacks.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The WMDP results vary by unlearning loss and method; VILA BIO accuracy ranges from 0.35 to 0.48, while its CYB accuracy ranges from 0.41 to 0.43.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "On Zephyr-7B-β, the best runs keeping at least 95% utility left WMDP accuracy above chance. VILA reached BIO 0.35–0.48 against 0.64 original. CYB barely changed: 0.41–0.43 against 0.44 original.",
        "proposed_claim": "LoRA-based unlearning (GD/NPO/IHL, with or without FILA or VILA) under a 95%-utility constraint lowers WMDP-Bio accuracy only partially and leaves WMDP-Cyber nearly unchanged.",
